# Sandbox relay (spec §6) — bridges the worker's outbound Attach stream to in-cluster/VM
# SandboxExec calls and mirrors connected workers into the Redis pool.

# SH_RELAY_PORT is the relay's own ATTACH bind port (packages/sandbox-relay/src/main.ts defaults
# it to 8443 if unset): sandbox containers dial it as RELAY_ADDR (setup-vm.sh's start_sandboxes
# reads it back with relay_port()). It is a different wire from MOCA_RELAY_EXEC_ADDR below, which
# the harness dials instead (SH_RELAY_ADDR in supervisor.env.example, harness/src/select-sandbox.ts)
# -- a change to one without the other silently breaks the wire it belongs to.
SH_RELAY_PORT=9443

# The relay serves SandboxExec (the workers' side) on its OWN listener, on loopback, so sandbox
# containers -- which reach the host's attach port -- cannot reach it at all (MI1 §5 R5/R8). The
# supervisor's SH_RELAY_ADDR dials this address; the two ports must agree.
MOCA_RELAY_EXEC_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9444

# Same Redis the supervisor uses (RedisRecordStore's own default, harness/src/pool-records.ts).
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379

# How long a detach mark (sh:sandbox:detached:<id>) lives; the affinity grace is measured from it
# (P6.3). Must match supervisor.env's SH_SANDBOX_AFFINITY_TTL_SECONDS.
#SH_SANDBOX_AFFINITY_TTL_SECONDS=86400

# Token validation is fail-closed (makeDefaultValidateToken in
# packages/sandbox-relay/src/main.ts): with nothing set, every attach is rejected, including a
# tokenless one. Set this to a shared secret matching the worker's SANDBOX_TOKEN before any
# sandbox can attach -- until then, attaches fail closed, not open.
#
# Left commented out, not empty, unlike SH_TURNS_PER_WORKER= above in the supervisor template:
# readConfig throws on a blank SH_TURNS_PER_WORKER, so empty is a loud failure there, whereas
# an empty SH_RELAY_TOKEN= here would be silently accepted as the literal empty string --
# which is a valid (and useless) token, not a missing one.
#SH_RELAY_TOKEN=

# The WORKERS' credential for the relay's SandboxExec (MI1 §5 R5), distinct from SH_RELAY_TOKEN,
# which sandboxes hold. The relay refuses to boot without it (unless SH_SANDBOX_TOKEN_PUBLIC_KEYS and
# SH_SANDBOX_TOKEN_ISSUER are set, in which case it boots on the sandbox keyset alone) and the supervisor's
# workers send it on every exec, so the two files must carry the SAME value. setup-vm.sh generates it into
# both when absent; never give it to a sandbox container.
#MOCA_RELAY_EXEC_TOKEN=

# SBX0 (sandbox service, spec §3): the sandbox control plane's PUBLIC keys, as
# <kid>:<base64 SPKI>[,<kid>:<base64 SPKI>...] (the format of SH_SESSION_TOKEN_PUBLIC_KEYS), and the
# issuer its tokens carry. With these set the relay accepts per-sandbox moca-sandbox+jwt tokens, and
# it boots without MOCA_RELAY_EXEC_TOKEN; with neither credential it refuses to boot. A keyset that
# does not parse, or one without its issuer, is a boot error naming the variable.
#SH_SANDBOX_TOKEN_PUBLIC_KEYS=
#SH_SANDBOX_TOKEN_ISSUER=sbx-control-plane

# The most unfinished Execs one sandbox (a token's sbx) may have in flight at once: one running and
# one waiting at the worker's per-workspace gate. A positive integer, default 2; anything else is a
# boot error, even on a harness-only relay. It applies only to sandbox callers: harness Execs are
# never capped.
#SH_SANDBOX_MAX_INFLIGHT=2
